The Failure Returned: Build a Reviewed QA Lesson

**Status:** draft requiring manual review before recording or publication  
**Product lane:** ProChat Memory for QA  
**Bounded outcome:** investigate one controlled test failure, approve one local lesson, then verify it against a later failure

Today I’m going to create one controlled test failure.

Then I’m going to investigate it calmly.

And finally, I’m going to show what happens when a similar failure returns later.

The result is one reviewed lesson in ProChat Memory for QA.

That lesson helps narrow a later investigation.

Current evidence still decides whether it applies.

My name is Steve.

I spent more than 12 years testing software in real environments.

The useful part of an investigation is not only the final fix.

It is the evidence, the ruled-out ideas, the cause, and the conditions that made the lesson true.

For this demonstration, I use a sanitized local test project.

There are no client names.

There are no private URLs.

There are no credentials.

The project memory stays local.

Let’s start with the result.

Here is the reviewed memory entry.

It records the observed failure.

It links to the evidence used during the investigation.

It states the confirmed cause.

It records one plausible idea that was ruled out.

And it includes a verification note for the next time a similar failure appears.

Now let’s reproduce the investigation.

The test currently passes.

I make one controlled change in the sanitized example so that the expected selector no longer matches.

Then I run the test again.

The test fails.

That failure is an observed fact.

The first interpretation might be that the page loaded too slowly.

But that is only a hypothesis.

I do not store it as a lesson yet.

I collect the current evidence.

The failure message.

The trace.

The screenshot.

And the current test code.

The evidence shows that the page loaded.

The expected element is absent under the old selector.

The current page contains the same element under a changed attribute.

Now I test the timing hypothesis.

I rerun with an explicit wait in the sanitized example.

The failure remains.

That rules out the timing explanation for this case.

I update the selector to match the current page.

Then I rerun the test.

It passes.

Now I can draft the lesson.

The draft says what failed.

It identifies the evidence.

It states that the selector changed.

It records that additional waiting did not resolve the failure.

It describes the fix.

And it tells the future tester what to verify before reusing the lesson.

Before approval, I review the draft manually.

I check that the evidence supports the stated cause.

I remove project-specific details that are not needed.

I confirm that no private information remains.

Then I approve the lesson into local memory.

That review step matters.

Raw notes are not trusted memory automatically.

Now we move forward in time.

A later test fails with a similar message.

ProChat Memory for QA retrieves the reviewed lesson.

The lesson suggests checking whether the selector changed again.

That is useful guidance.

It is not a diagnosis.

I collect fresh evidence from the later failure.

This time the selector still matches.

The trace shows that the element never appeared because the expected test data was missing.

The old lesson does not apply.

Current evidence overrides it.

I do not force the failure into the old explanation.

I record that the lesson was considered and rejected for this case.

The original lesson can remain valid for its original conditions.

The later investigation needs its own reviewed conclusion.

That is the control point.

Memory narrows the search.

Evidence decides the cause.

Human review decides what becomes trusted memory.

The visible validation for this video is clear.

The controlled failure is reproducible.

The first fix makes the test pass.

The lesson preserves its evidence and review state.

The later failure retrieves the lesson.

Fresh evidence correctly rejects that lesson as the cause.

The limitation is also clear.

One useful retrieval does not prove that every stored lesson will be relevant or correct.

Lessons can become outdated.

They may need correction or retirement.

The next action is simple.

Try one recurring failure with ProChat Memory for QA.

Start with sanitized evidence.

Review the lesson before approval.

And verify it again when the failure returns.

Before recording or publishing this script, every failure, trace, screenshot, memory entry, and sanitization step must be reviewed manually.
